uli_ids": [], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " Getting deep o the basic elements.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Take Away", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "allowed_attempts": 2}, "type": "feedback"}, {"type": "learning_skills", "contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}]}, {"type": "post_instructions", "contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}]}], "id": 11, "type": "DWS"}], "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "text": "Good morning, everybody. Before we talk about common programming languages, I would rather start by talking about the five basic elements that have made programming possible.\nTo start with, you always need to have an input. The input is the information that enters into the system and is needed for a program to work. Programmers have used keyboards, text files, and touchscreens as ways of entering data into a program. The next element is arithmetic. This has always been the most difficult one as it is where all the mathematical operations happen. Also, if the program doesn't directly need operations, it might still need them for the programming itself.", "id": 0, "image": "enpe7n5le01ob01re2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "47000", "text": "The next step is the output. Programming itself has been designed for the purpose of having an outcome. After the processing of the input has been done, the sole purpose of a program is to give a result. This way, the outcome might be the most tangible part of programming.", "id": 1, "image": "enpe7n5le01ob01re2img02.jpg"}, {"time": "68600", "text": "When deciding what kind of looping you would rather use in order to repeat an operation, a number in the program is an essential part of programming. Would you rather use the event-controlled loop or the counter-controlled loop? You have to check which is best in order for it to suit your needs. Do you need this eternal loop to be stopped by an external event or by a predetermined counter? Always ask yourself what is best for the program. Don't forget that an important element for the loop is the conditional, which determines when the loop finishes. Finally, people have always needed maps to guide themselves through processes. In this case, you need a flowchart to make sure you are following the right steps in a process.", "id": 2, "image": "enpe7n5le01ob01re2img03.jpg"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le01ob01re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le01ob01re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "title": "", "id": 1, "type": "audio"}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}], "type": "sena"}